WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.504
Embedded threat modeling is a structured
way to make assumptions visible.

2
00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:08.143
A useful model identifies device roles,
normal users, operators,

3
00:00:08.223 --> 00:00:12.481
service technicians, manufacturing steps,
connected systems, update paths,

4
00:00:12.561 --> 00:00:15.103
and the data or commands that move between
them.

5
00:00:15.183 --> 00:00:17.408
The goal is not to create a dramatic
story;

6
00:00:17.488 --> 00:00:19.776
it is to make engineering review concrete.

7
00:00:19.856 --> 00:00:22.207
Trust boundaries can appear in many
places.

8
00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:26.227
Physical access, network access, debug
access, firmware update access,

9
00:00:26.307 --> 00:00:30.756
peripheral access, removable media, local
configuration, manufacturing tools,

10
00:00:30.836 --> 00:00:34.522
and cloud or service connections can all
influence device behavior.

11
00:00:34.602 --> 00:00:38.416
Each boundary should have an owner and a
clear validation expectation.

12
00:00:38.496 --> 00:00:40.656
Resource constraints shape the design.

13
00:00:40.736 --> 00:00:44.931
CPU time, RAM, flash, power budget,
real-time deadlines, watchdog behavior,

14
00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:49.333
storage wear, and communication timing can
limit which controls are practical.

15
00:00:49.413 --> 00:00:53.417
A secure design should fit the device
rather than pretending the device has

16
00:00:53.497 --> 00:00:56.738
unlimited memory, processing time, or
maintenance access.

17
00:00:56.818 --> 00:00:58.661
Deployment environment matters.

18
00:00:58.741 --> 00:01:02.109
A device in a locked cabinet, a vehicle, a
hospital room, a home,

19
00:01:02.189 --> 00:01:06.447
a factory floor, or a remote outdoor
installation faces different maintenance

20
00:01:06.527 --> 00:01:08.561
workflows and physical assumptions.

21
00:01:08.641 --> 00:01:13.026
Those assumptions should be documented so
future developers understand what the

22
00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:14.950
product was designed to tolerate.

23
00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.906
Controls should match product risk, safety
impact, update capability,

24
00:01:18.986 --> 00:01:20.385
and deployment lifetime.

25
00:01:20.465 --> 00:01:22.880
A short-lived prototype, a consumer
sensor,

26
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.137
and an industrial controller may need
different controls,

27
00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:28.378
but all of them need explicit decisions.

28
00:01:28.458 --> 00:01:32.907
The dangerous pattern is assuming that a
constraint or an environment makes review

29
00:01:32.987 --> 00:01:33.750
unnecessary.

30
00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.990
Threat modeling also supports testing.

31
00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.884
Once assumptions are visible, teams can
write tests for invalid input,

32
00:01:39.964 --> 00:01:44.285
missing configuration, service-mode
behavior, reset timing, update failure,

33
00:01:44.365 --> 00:01:47.416
power interruption, and production build
differences.

34
00:01:47.496 --> 00:01:49.847
The model becomes a checklist for
evidence.

35
00:01:49.927 --> 00:01:52.342
A good embedded threat model is kept
current.

36
00:01:52.422 --> 00:01:55.791
Manufacturing changes, new interfaces,
dependency updates,

37
00:01:55.871 --> 00:02:00.256
revised service workflows, and product
variants can all change the assumptions.

38
00:02:00.336 --> 00:02:04.340
Treat the model as living engineering
documentation, not a one-time form.
